### `sql` leaves the ordinary tool surface; history tools follow the Run (`implemented` 2026-10-05, offline evidence only)

[The decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-05-sql-leaves-the-ordinary-tool-surface.md) closes the first gap slice 2 of M11 recorded. Reproduced first: from another Workspace's conversation and from a temporary one, `sql` returned a sentence said in Workspace P while `memory_search` did not. `sql` is out of the built-in `research` Preset and out of `all`; it exists only where the user's own list names it. `memory_search`, `tool_history`, `file_history` and `recent_errors` read for the conversation of the Run that called them, and search nothing when there is none, where they used to read the current buffer's conversation and fall back to every conversation. `scripts/workspace-scope-regression.el` 9 tests, 6 red first and 3 guards; the recall, composition, sub-agent rules, work-record, timer and permission suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. No real-model run. Still open from slice 2: the user's `/recall` searches every conversation, and a workflow step carried no rules (closed below, "Rules on rewrite and workflow").

### A wake that goes wrong (`implemented` 2026-10-06, offline evidence only)

Fault injection first: `scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el`, 8 tests, 5 red on the code as it stood (a wake whose send signalled, a wake cut off before the send, two due records with the first failing, the report after the in-memory link was lost) and 3 guards. The repair is in `superchat-work.el` only and adds no queue and no scheduler: a wake counts when a Run started in its conversation, read from the Tape; a wait whose wake started nothing is put back, at once when the send signals and at the next scan when Emacs had exited; the report to the main conversation is found from the `work.woken` fact instead of an in-memory table. 2 more tests after 7 mutants, of which 5 were red and 2 survived until those tests were added; 10 tests in all. The work-record, timer, history, scope and composition suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. No real-model run. The three things left open here are closed by the next entry.

### Work that came due and did not start (`implemented` 2026-10-06, offline evidence only)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-06-a-fired-task-that-did-not-start.md`. Timer tasks: `timer.sending` and `timer.started` record the send and the Run; a fired task without a Run is started later (retry in the same Emacs, and first thing after a restart); a send that may have been cut off is recorded as `timer.unconfirmed`, said in the conversation and not repeated; a busy conversation makes the task wait instead of queueing in memory; a fired, unstarted task can be cancelled. `scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 12 tests; they call functions the old code does not have, so all 12 fail there and that count says little. 11 mutants of the repair: 10 red, 1 survived (the `timer.sending` fact not written) until an assertion was added. Work Records: a failed wake is retried in the same Emacs (300 s, 3 times); an execution an earlier Emacs never finished gets the outcome `interrupted` and one line in the main conversation, and is not started again. 4 more tests in `scripts/work-wake-fault-regression.el` (14 in all), 3 red on the code as it stood and 1 guard. No queue and no scheduler were added. The code was written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order and is byte-identical to the leader's prototype. No real-model run; an exit of Emacs is simulated by not sending and by hand-written facts, not by killing a process. Still open: what a cut-off execution left in unsaved buffers (step 4 of the plan).

### Rules on rewrite and workflow (`implemented` 2026-10-06; the rewrite measured on real models 2026-10-07)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-06-rules-on-rewrite-and-workflow.md`. Reproduced first by reading the request sent to the provider: a region rewrite carried only its own system text, a workflow step no system text at all. The one rules collector now also serves `inline-rewrite` and `workflow` (rules alone, no note about remembering; the rewrite's own instruction stays last); compaction and skill matching carry none. A rules file that exists and cannot be read is told to the user, and to the model on the chat surfaces; the request is not refused. `scripts/entry-rules-regression.el`, 12 tests; of the first 11, 8 were red on the code as it stood and 3 are guards. 8 mutants, all red. The rules, sub-agent-rules, rewrite, composition, history and scope suites, the M3 set, the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green. Written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order, byte-identical to the leader's prototype. Measured on real models on 2026-10-07 with a rule "start every answer with a line of reasoning": placing the rewrite's instruction last was not enough, DeepSeek put that line into the user's file 3 times of 3 (local Qwen 0 of 1). The rules of a rewrite are now followed by a note saying which kind of rule applies to it (`superchat-prompt--rewrite-rules-note`); after it DeepSeek 0 of 5 and Qwen 0 of 3 carried the line, and the date-format rule in the same file was followed 4 of 5 and 3 of 3. One scenario and one conflicting rule; the suite is 13 tests, the new one red on the code before and on two mutants. Still open: the Telega and emacsclient entries are untraced; a queued input after a window switch has no test of its own; a workflow step is still not recorded as conversation.

### Working beside the user: what real runs showed (2026-10-07, observations before the repairs below)

Twelve scenarios in a sandboxed Emacs with a stand-in user (`.scratch/task-sim/harness-goals/tasks-step4.el`, not in the repository), DeepSeek three times each and local Qwen once, after a first round that was used to correct the scenarios' own checks and is not counted. Counts are runs. They are small; a scenario seen once on Qwen is one run.

Held every time, both models:

- The user and the agent edit one buffer in turn: the user's edits and the agent's are all there (3 and 1).
- A Run in progress when the user switches to another Workspace's conversation, or changes preset: it completes, its edit lands, its answer is in its own conversation and nothing of it in the other (3 and 1 each).
- An approval question left unanswered for 75 seconds: Emacs keeps running timers, and after "no" the call is not run and the answer does not invent a result (3 and 1).
- A record that wakes while the user is typing a draft: the draft's text and point are unchanged (3 and 1).
- The user has unsaved edits and another program changes the file: the user's line and the other program's line both survive the agent's edit (3 and 1, in each of two variants).
- After a restart the record is found in the Workspace, carried into the new conversation, and finished there (2 and 1; a third DeepSeek run left no result file).

Did not hold:

- **A woken record's review screen takes the cursor** out of the draft the user is typing in: 2 of 3 on DeepSeek, 1 of 1 on Qwen. This is the review screen's accepted behaviour applied to work nobody is watching; whether automatic work may take focus is the user's open decision.
- **Killing the conversation's buffer while its Run works fails the Run** (3 of 3, 1 of 1): the edit is not made, the reason recorded is "Human input adapter is no longer available".
- **The agent is never told the file changed on disk** (0 of 8 runs). When the buffer had no unsaved edits before the outside change, Emacs asks its own "changed on disk; really edit the buffer?" during the agent's edit (4 of 4); Superchat neither asks it nor knows of it.
- **A Work Record's rendering does not name its executions**, only the files they changed (0 of 3): "which run did this" is answerable from the Tape, not from the record the user sees.
- **Two Runs appending to one buffer**: no line was lost or doubled and each run saw the other's lines, but on DeepSeek, in 2 of 3 attempts one of the two Runs ended one line short with an answer that announced the next line instead of saying it stopped. Cause not established. On Qwen the second concurrent request got an error body from the local server in a shape the client did not parse, and that Run never ended (waited 240 s).
- **An approval question that stands** blocks the minibuffer for everything. A record that came due meanwhile woke and made its edit (3 of 3 on DeepSeek in a rerun with the scenario corrected, 1 of 1 on Qwen); in those three reruns the woken Run then asked its own question, apparently on top of the standing one (the snapshot shows a second minibuffer), and neither moved until the stand-in answered at 300 s.

Not exercised: a human at the keyboard; killing Emacs in mid-Run (the restart scenario restarts between Runs); more than two concurrent Runs; Telega and emacsclient.

### Working beside the user: the three rulings and what was repaired (`implemented`, 2026-10-07)

Decision: `docs/decisions/accepted/2026-10-07-working-beside-the-user.md` (the user, 2026-10-07: "按照建议"). An execution nobody started (a scheduled task's, a woken record's) shows no review screen and so does not take the cursor; the user's own executions keep the 2026-09-25 behaviour. A Run whose conversation buffer is killed goes on and fails only the `ask_user` call that needs the user. Before a structured edit Superchat looks whether the file changed on disk: a buffer without unsaved changes is reloaded and the edit sent back to be redone, a buffer with unsaved changes is edited and the model told that saving would overwrite the disk; `read_buffer` says so too; the user gets one echo-area line. A Work Record opened from the list names its executions (the text sent to the model is unchanged). A server error llm.el cannot read becomes an ordinary error instead of a Run that never ends. `scripts/beside-user-regression.el`, 13 tests; of the first 11, 9 were red on the code as it stood and 2 are guards; 14 mutants, all red. Every focused suite, the M3 set (137 expected, 2 skipped, untouched), the canonical smoke and the Kernel check stay green; byte-compilation prints the same 79 warning lines before and after. Written by pi with deepseek-flash from a find/replace work order, identical to the leader's prototype.

Real runs after the change (DeepSeek three times, Qwen once, same scenarios): the cursor stayed in the draft 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the Run with its buffer killed completed 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the model was told of the disk change and told the user 6 of 6 and 2 of 2, and Emacs's own question appeared 0 of 4; the record named the execution before the restart 3 of 3 and 1 of 1; the Qwen Run that used to hang ended with the server's error (1 of 1). The first batch of the disk scenarios was misjudged by the harness (it took Superchat's own echo-area line for a question) and was rerun.

Two Runs on one buffer, one ending a line short: cause established from the provider's raw replies in both failing runs. The short Run's last reply was text only ("Second line added. Now the third."), `finish_reason` `stop`, no tool call; a reply without a tool call is the final answer. Superchat dropped no tool call and no line. Nothing was changed for it.

Still open: an automatic execution's unsaved changes are announced by one echo-area line only; edits made through `eval_elisp` or a shell do not pass the disk check; a standing approval question with a second one on top (seen in the step-4 runs) is untouched; no human at the keyboard.

### Minimum Emacs 30.1 (`implemented`, verified 2026-09-23)

[The minimum Emacs is 30.1](decisions/implemented/2026-09-22-minimum-emacs-30-1.md), the user's ruling of 2026-09-22 after the Emacs 29.4 run. On a locally built 30.1 every suite, the canonical smoke, the kernel check and a real DeepSeek chat session (read, edit, undo, refused `eval_elisp`) pass; the run found one product fault, C-g not cancelling an approval question asked from a callback, which also affected 30.1 and is fixed for every supported Emacs. Evidence: `.scratch/g3-emacs30/README.md`. Not verified: a GUI Emacs 30.1, Emacs 30.2.

### Skills, models and commands anywhere in a message (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-triggers-anywhere-in-the-message.md). `>skill`, `@model` and `/command` are read from the middle of a message the user typed, there only when the name exists; what opens the message reads as before and wins; a message a timer or a woken Work Record sends is read at its start only; a model name is checked without asking the provider. One parse point (`superchat-core--parse-input`), no second path. Evidence: `scripts/input-syntax-regression.el`, 18 tests; 12 fail on the code before the change, 6 are guards; 12 of 12 mutations caught. Not verified: a real model, the completion-then-send flow by hand in a live Emacs. Not built: a skill that applies to one request only (undecided), the other items learned from the gptel presets talk ([proposal](decisions/proposed/2026-10-10-source-turn-destination.md)).

### An agent skill for one message (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-an-agent-skill-for-one-message.md). An `agent` skill named in the middle of a message shapes that one request (persona, tools, model) and leaves the Session's preset and Tape configuration alone; at the start it still switches the Session; a `plan` skill switches wherever it is named. The one request carries the skill's own Capability, because Runtime refuses a request whose Capability differs from its Agent Profile's. Evidence: `scripts/skill-once-regression.el`, 5 tests through the real send path; 3 fail on the code before the change, 2 are guards; 6 of 6 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, three messages (plain, agent skill named mid-message, plain): DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 followed the skill on that message and dropped it on the next, with the Session not switched; the task names the buffer to read. Not done: the skill's `plugins:` are not mounted for the one message. Found then, repaired in the next entry: a message that invokes any skill, at the start or in the middle, goes out without the `[Emacs origin]` block and the window arrangement, because a skill-fixed prompt travels as `:chat-prompt-override`, which those collectors read as "the user already previewed this text"; one earlier DeepSeek run that did not name the buffer answered that it had no information.

### A skill message says where the user is (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-a-skill-message-says-where-the-user-is.md). The origin and window-arrangement collectors stay silent only for a request the user previewed (`:chat-prepared-context`); a message whose text a skill fixed (`:chat-prompt-override` alone) carries the `[Emacs origin]` block, the explicit selection and `[Emacs windows]` like any other. Evidence: `scripts/skill-context-regression.el`, 5 tests through the real send path; 4 fail on the code before the change, 1 is a guard; 4 of 4 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, a skill message that does not name the buffer ("用一句话说明 >duty 这里写的周五要交什么。"): DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 answered from the buffer the user was in; before the change one DeepSeek run on such a message answered that it had no information.

### A notice for work nobody watched (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-a-notice-for-work-nobody-watched.md). An execution Superchat started by itself (a scheduled task, a woken Work Record) leaves a notice when it ends, however it ends; the mode line shows `[Superchat: N]` until the user opens or dismisses the notices (`superchat-changes-notices`). Hooked on `turn-completed` inside the existing `changes` plugin, using the Tape-derived `superchat-changes--unattended-run-p`; no Sink was wrapped and no plugin row added. The notices live in this Emacs only. Evidence: `scripts/unattended-notice-regression.el`, 8 tests, the task and the wake on the real path; 11 of 11 mutations caught. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, stand-in user, a Work Record that wakes after two minutes while the user types a draft: DeepSeek 3/3 and local Qwen 1/1 left exactly one notice naming the record and showed `[Superchat: 1]`, with the draft and the cursor untouched. Three more runs of that scenario gave no result; they ran at the same time as the full check suite and the same scenario passed when run alone, so they are counted as interference between parallel runs, by timing only -- how they interfered was not found.

### Remarks hung on the text (`implemented`, 2026-10-10)

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-remarks-hung-on-the-text.md). `superchat-annotate` asks for remarks on the region or the buffer and hangs each on the text it is about, as overlays; the text is never changed. The model sees numbered lines and answers `LINE | QUOTE | REMARK`; a remark is placed where its quote is found on that line or within two lines, inside the region, and nowhere otherwise. Chosen after an experiment outside Superchat (570 rows, two models): quoting alone let the local Qwen invent 11 of 27 quotes on code, line number plus quote placed 21 of 21. Its own surface `inline-annotate`, registered by the existing `inline-rewrite` plugin row; rules carried with their own note; no tools. The transport's stand-in for an empty answer is now the constant `superchat-llm-empty-response-text`. Evidence: `scripts/annotate-regression.el`, 18 tests through a real send to a scripted model; 27 mutations, 25 caught, the other two are the two "no tools" settings that back each other. Real models through the command, sandboxed Emacs: DeepSeek 3/3 on configuration (32 of 32 each), notes, and notes under a rule asking for a line of reasoning first (never written into the answer); on code 13 to 20 remarks placed per run with 0 to 6 not placed, mostly quotes written as string literals. Local Qwen 1/1 on configuration, notes and notes under the rule; on code nothing with thinking on (output spent on reasoning at the server's 8192 context), 9 of 12 and 6 of 299 rows with thinking off. Found on the way: the harness's `thinking: disabled` is ignored by LM Studio, so every earlier local-Qwen run had thinking on. Display changed afterwards at the user's request, without Flymake: a remark is a block under its line, wrapped and indented like the line, the remarks of one line stacked in text order (6 more tests, 24 in all; 10 mutations of the display code, all caught; seen only as printed text, not in a window). Not solved: string-literal quotes and quotes holding a bar are not placed; the message for an answer cut at its length limit; no cap on a long answer; no person at the keyboard.

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-annotations-asked-for-in-a-conversation.md). In a conversation, `>annotate` makes that one message's answer also hang on the buffer the conversation was called from (or the buffer or file named after it): history carried, no tools, the conversation's own Sink wrapped and not replaced, the row form last in the system text. The mark is a destination recorded on the Turn, read at the one parse point before `>skill`, never read from a message Superchat sent itself. The Tape keeps the mark on such a message (the model is sent the text without it), so regenerate annotates again and edit restores the mark; a later message of that conversation is told the earlier answer was hung on the buffer and is finished. The note is written once a remark is on the text and is kept with its Session id, so a failed request, an answer that placed nothing, or another conversation in the same View is not told. Evidence: `scripts/annotate-chat-regression.el`, 19 tests through a real conversation with a scripted model, one of them the smoke's request-rebuild verdict on an annotating message; 29 mutations, all caught; `scripts/smoke-expected/V.txt` now counts 15 collectors. Real models through a conversation, sandboxed Emacs, three messages from 15 lines of meeting notes (a question, "mark these on the notes `>annotate`", an ordinary one): the annotating message placed every remark on its exact line in every run (DeepSeek 10 runs, local Qwen 3). Before the "it was hung" note DeepSeek answered the ordinary message after it by writing the remarks into the buffer in 1 of 3 runs; with the note, 0 of 7. Small samples; the local Qwen with thinking on did not reach its context limit here. Not solved: the "it was hung" note lives in the View, for this Emacs only; the rows stay in the conversation and its history; no person at the keyboard.

[Decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-work-records-and-timers-belong-to-a-workspace.md). The architecture review of 2026-10-10 (`reports/architecture-review-main-2026-10-10/`) was re-run on the day's code: of its findings F1, F2, F4, F5, N1 and N2 all still reproduced. F1 and F2 are closed here: the model's `work_record` resolves a record through `superchat-work--for`, and `timer_list` / `timer_cancel` go through `superchat-timer--within-p`; another Workspace's record or timer is refused, a conversation of no Workspace reaches only what was made in it, and the user's own list and commands and the product's own writes are unchanged. A sub-agent's conversation is bound to no Workspace, so its own tool call on the record is refused, as K14 already told it. Evidence: `scripts/object-scope-regression.el`, 15 tests (two through the real tool loop), 9 red on the old code; 16 mutations, all caught; the review's two probes for these findings now fail. Real models, sandboxed Emacs, scenario OSC (alpha starts a record and sets a reminder; beta is given the record's id and asked to complete it, then asked to list and cancel every timer): DeepSeek 2 of 2 and the local Qwen 1 of 1 were refused, said so to the user without claiming success, were shown neither the goal nor the reminder, and left both untouched. N1 is closed: a scheduled task's retry limit and its own wait after a failure are now checked before a start is tried, not only when the next retry is scheduled, and the one retry timer is due at the soonest time any task asked for; before, a task whose conversation stayed busy kept that timer going and carried a failing task past its limit (6 sends at a limit of 2 in the review's probe) and brought its retries forward to every 30 seconds. Evidence: `scripts/timer-start-fault-regression.el`, 15 tests (3 new, 2 changed to move the clock past the wait before retrying); 9 mutations, all caught; the review's N1 probe now fails. A Work Record's wake has its own timer per record and did not have this fault. N2 is closed: a scheduled task whose model answered inside the send had ended before `timer.started` was written, was judged the user's own execution, and left no notice on the mode line; the timer now tells through `superchat-timer-started-functions` once the fact is written and the changes plugin leaves the notice of an execution that has already ended, still once. The review screen was not affected: it is decided from a timer, after the send returned. A Work Record's wake writes `work.woken` before it sends and did not have this fault. Evidence: `scripts/unattended-notice-regression.el`, 11 tests (3 new, with the model answering inside the send); 5 mutations, all caught; the review's N2 probe now fails. The documents the review named as describing history are corrected in `docs/architecture.md` (which Run a woken record started; what writing "fired" first does and does not promise; the mode-line notice) and `CONTEXT.md` (which conversation an in-place command joins). F4 and F5 are closed ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-the-work-record-tool-keeps-or-refuses.md)): a `start` that comes with progress, a place to pick up and a wait records them with the start, and one that cannot keep what it came with starts nothing; a wait for a time is refused, with the reason, while the `work-records` row is not mounted, and reading, updating, waiting for the user and completing still work; an `update` checks its wait before it writes. Evidence: `scripts/work-tool-regression.el`, 10 tests, 7 red on the old code; 10 mutations, all caught; the review's F4 and F5 probes now fail, so of its seven probes only the F3 guard still passes. The same question for the `timers` row was then asked and had the same answer: unmounted, `timer_create` answered "the user will be reminded" and wrote a timer nothing would fire. It is now refused with the reason and writes nothing; `timer_list` says timers are off; `timer_cancel` is unchanged ([decision](decisions/accepted/2026-10-10-the-timer-tool-sets-or-refuses.md); `scripts/timer-tool-regression.el`, 5 tests, 4 red on the old code; 6 mutations, all caught). Not done from the review: an explicit dependency between a capability's tool, collector, observer and timers; plugins a one-message skill declares; the "later" view; narrow tools measured on the local model.

### Fixes from the 2026-09-23 round (`implemented`)
